WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.590
Instructor: So next up, I'd like to talk about

00:01.590 --> 00:04.110
the course curriculum and what we cover.

00:04.110 --> 00:07.170
And I'll try to break it down into some smaller pieces,

00:07.170 --> 00:10.290
because on the surface we cover quite a few topics

00:10.290 --> 00:12.540
related to Git and GitHub.

00:12.540 --> 00:15.510
So there's always a pressure, honestly, in these courses

00:15.510 --> 00:19.950
to fit as many related topics as possible into a course

00:19.950 --> 00:23.370
just to compete or to stand out in some way.

00:23.370 --> 00:25.320
And, yeah, I can't ignore that,

00:25.320 --> 00:28.620
but I do wanna try my best to make it very clear

00:28.620 --> 00:31.590
what actually matters and what is fundamental.

00:31.590 --> 00:34.020
Because not all of this stuff you see in front of you,

00:34.020 --> 00:35.460
and there's likely to be more

00:35.460 --> 00:37.440
by the time you're actually watching this,

00:37.440 --> 00:39.630
there will be more videos or more additional sections

00:39.630 --> 00:42.360
added towards the end because students request them.

00:42.360 --> 00:45.780
But anyway, not all of these are critical.

00:45.780 --> 00:47.850
So there's a couple of chunks, I guess,

00:47.850 --> 00:49.740
that I would break things down into.

00:49.740 --> 00:52.770
The first bit is all about the core functionality of Git.

00:52.770 --> 00:54.840
Working with the very, very basics.

00:54.840 --> 00:56.430
Things like adding and committing,

00:56.430 --> 00:59.460
and creating repositories, git log, git status,

00:59.460 --> 01:02.850
creating branches, checking out, switching, merging.

01:02.850 --> 01:05.130
That is your core Git toolbox.

01:05.130 --> 01:08.790
And then we talked about, I'm calling it Next Level Git,

01:08.790 --> 01:11.190
but it's not crazy difficult stuff.

01:11.190 --> 01:12.810
It's just a couple of Git topics

01:12.810 --> 01:14.910
that won't come up as often.

01:14.910 --> 01:17.460
They're not part of your day-to-day Git life,

01:17.460 --> 01:19.200
but they still are very important.

01:19.200 --> 01:22.980
So we talk about diff-ing, the git diff command, stashing.

01:22.980 --> 01:25.020
And then we talk about quite a few ways

01:25.020 --> 01:28.080
of time traveling in Git, or undoing changes.

01:28.080 --> 01:31.200
Git revert, git reset, git restore,

01:31.200 --> 01:33.750
undoing changes, and going back to old work

01:33.750 --> 01:36.240
and detaching and reattaching the head.

01:36.240 --> 01:39.030
And there's quite a bit of stuff in that section.

01:39.030 --> 01:42.870
So it's important, but it's just not part of this Git core.

01:42.870 --> 01:46.380
Then we move on to GitHub and collaboration.

01:46.380 --> 01:48.660
So I've grouped together four sections here.

01:48.660 --> 01:50.130
I don't really have a nice title for it,

01:50.130 --> 01:52.650
but we basically learn about the basics of GitHub,

01:52.650 --> 01:55.530
working with GitHub, getting our code up on GitHub,

01:55.530 --> 01:57.240
getting stuff down from GitHub.

01:57.240 --> 02:01.110
We talk about things like GitHub pages and GitHub gists,

02:01.110 --> 02:03.120
and collaboration workflows.

02:03.120 --> 02:05.640
That's a very significant chunk of this course.

02:05.640 --> 02:06.990
We spend a lot of time on that,

02:06.990 --> 02:08.370
talking about the different ways

02:08.370 --> 02:11.850
that you could use Git with a team to collaborate,

02:11.850 --> 02:14.100
along with GitHub, and different structures,

02:14.100 --> 02:15.540
different strategies you can employ.

02:15.540 --> 02:16.890
There's not just one way.

02:16.890 --> 02:19.320
And they all have some certain strengths and weaknesses.

02:19.320 --> 02:20.520
So I demonstrate those

02:20.520 --> 02:22.110
and spend a lot of time talking about it.

02:22.110 --> 02:23.670
It's pretty important stuff.

02:23.670 --> 02:25.710
So I've grouped that together with GitHub.

02:25.710 --> 02:29.310
And then after that, it's kind of just a mixture

02:29.310 --> 02:33.270
of other Git stuff that I felt compelled to include.

02:33.270 --> 02:35.160
And these are some of the more advanced topics

02:35.160 --> 02:36.600
or the niche topics.

02:36.600 --> 02:38.850
So this includes things like rebasing,

02:38.850 --> 02:41.520
cleaning up your Git history with interactive rebasing,

02:41.520 --> 02:44.910
squashing, dropping, fixing up.

02:44.910 --> 02:47.670
All sorts of fun parts, in my opinion.

02:47.670 --> 02:48.960
We talk about tags.

02:48.960 --> 02:53.160
We talk about how Git works behind the scenes and hashing.

02:53.160 --> 02:57.330
We cover things like git objects, git blobs, git trees.

02:57.330 --> 02:58.350
So there's stuff in here

02:58.350 --> 03:00.870
that you could go your whole life as a get user

03:00.870 --> 03:01.980
without knowing.

03:01.980 --> 03:04.440
I think it's worth giving you the option, at least,

03:04.440 --> 03:06.180
to check it out if you're curious.

03:06.180 --> 03:08.310
We talk about creating custom aliases

03:08.310 --> 03:10.620
and shortcuts and ref logs.

03:10.620 --> 03:11.550
That's a fun one.

03:11.550 --> 03:13.830
I'm happy with how that section turned out.

03:13.830 --> 03:15.660
And as I said, there's likely to be more.

03:15.660 --> 03:18.450
If you scroll down right now through the curriculum,

03:18.450 --> 03:20.040
there's likely more content in the course

03:20.040 --> 03:21.720
that's not included here.

03:21.720 --> 03:24.210
So to recap this, there's quite a bit,

03:24.210 --> 03:26.160
and it's all Git and GitHub oriented.

03:26.160 --> 03:27.750
But there's this first chunk

03:27.750 --> 03:30.240
that is the core of Git on its own.

03:30.240 --> 03:33.420
Then we talk about some next level Git topics

03:33.420 --> 03:35.790
that are absolutely important to know,

03:35.790 --> 03:38.760
but they're not part of your typical hour-to-hour

03:38.760 --> 03:42.270
or day-to-day use case for Git, or they might not be.

03:42.270 --> 03:45.270
And then we talk about GitHub and collaboration workflows.

03:45.270 --> 03:48.600
And then it's just a bunch of other Git related topics

03:48.600 --> 03:50.940
at the end that I had a lot of fun recording.

03:50.940 --> 03:52.653
Okay, so let's get started.
